According to Section 114 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, "Whoever causes bodily pain, disease or infirmity to any person is said to cause hurt."

On the other hand, Section 116 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 defines "grievous hurt." It states that the following kinds of hurt are designated as "grievous":

(a) Emasculation;
(b) Permanent privation of the sight of either eye;
(c) Permanent privation of the hearing of either ear;
(d) Privation of any member or joint;
(e) Destruction or permanent impairing of the powers of any member or joint;
(f) Permanent disfiguration of the head or face;
(g) Fracture or dislocation of a bone or tooth;
(h) Any hurt which endangers life or which causes the sufferer to be during the space of fifteen days in severe bodily pain, or unable to follow his ordinary pursuits.

In summary, hurt refers to any form of bodily pain, disease, or infirmity, whereas grievous hurt involves more serious injuries as listed above.

Culpable homicide refers to the act of causing someone's death through an intentional or knowingly dangerous action. According to Section 100 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, culpable homicide occurs when a person causes death by doing an act with the intention of causing death, or with the intention of causing such bodily injury as is likely to cause death, or with the knowledge that such act is likely to cause death.

Murder, as defined in Section 101 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, is a specific type of culpable homicide. It involves causing death with certain intentions or knowledge that go beyond what is required for culpable homicide. Specifically, murder is culpable homicide:

(a) if the act by which the death is caused is done with the intention of causing death;
(b) if the act by which the death is caused is done with the intention of causing such bodily injury as the offender knows to be likely to cause the death of the person to whom the harm is caused;
(c) if the act by which the death is caused is done with the intention of causing bodily injury to any person and the bodily injury intended to be inflicted is sufficient in the ordinary course of nature to cause death;
(d) if the person committing the act by which the death is caused, knows that it is so imminently dangerous that it must, in all probability, cause death, or such bodily injury as is likely to cause death, and commits such act without any excuse for incurring the risk of causing death or such injury as aforesaid.

In summary, while both culpable homicide and murder involve causing someone's death through an intentional or knowingly dangerous act, murder is characterized by a higher degree of intent or knowledge regarding the likelihood of causing death.

Free legal aid refers to the provision of legal services at no cost to individuals who qualify based on certain criteria. These services include the assistance of a lawyer, the cost of drafting and filing documents, and the payment of process and incidental costs. Under Section 13 of the Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987, persons who satisfy the criteria specified in Section 12 are entitled to receive these legal services.

According to Section 12 of the Act, the following categories of persons are entitled to free legal services regardless of their income:

(a) Members of a Scheduled Caste or Scheduled Tribe;
(b) Victims of trafficking in human beings or begar as referred to in Article 23 of the Constitution;
(c) Women and children;
(d) Persons with disabilities as defined in clause (i) of section 2 of the Persons With Disabilities (Equal Opportunities, Protection of Rights and Full Participation) Act, 1995;
(e) Persons under circumstances of underserved want such as being a victim of a mass disaster, ethnic violence, caste atrocity, flood, drought, earthquake, or industrial disaster;
(f) Industrial workmen;
(g) Persons in custody, including those in protective homes, juvenile homes, psychiatric hospitals, or psychiatric nursing homes.

Additionally, anyone else can be eligible for free legal aid if their annual income is below the limit prescribed by their State Legal Services Authority, as per Section 12(h) of the Act.

For more detailed information and to apply for free legal aid, you can contact the District Legal Services Authority (DLSA) in your district court complex, call the NALSA toll-free helpline 15100, or apply online through the National Legal Services Authority's portal.

According to Section 51A of the Constitution of India, the fundamental duties of citizens include:

(a) To abide by the Constitution and respect its ideals and institutions, the National Flag and the National Anthem;
(b) To cherish and follow the noble ideals which inspired our national struggle for freedom;
(c) To uphold and protect the sovereignty, unity and integrity of India;
(d) To defend the country and render national service when called upon to do so;
(e) To promote harmony and the spirit of common brotherhood amongst all the people of India transcending religious, linguistic and regional or sectional diversities; to renounce practices derogatory to the dignity of women;
(f) To value and preserve the rich heritage of our composite culture;
(g) To protect and improve the natural environment including forests, lakes, rivers and wildlife, and to have compassion for living creatures;
(h) To develop the scientific temper, humanism and the spirit of inquiry and reform;
(i) To safeguard public property and to abjure violence;
(j) To strive towards excellence in all spheres of individual and collective activity so that the nation constantly rises to higher levels of endeavor and achievement;
(k) Who is a parent or guardian to provide opportunities for education to his child or, as the case may be, ward between the age of six and fourteen years.

These duties are not enforceable by any court but are fundamental in the governance of the country, and it is the duty of the State to apply these principles in making laws.

A 'money bill' is a specific type of legislative document in India that deals exclusively with financial matters. According to Section 110 of the Constitution of India, a Bill shall be deemed to be a Money Bill if it contains only provisions dealing with all or any of the following matters:

(a) the imposition, abolition, remission, alteration or regulation of any tax;
(b) the regulation of the borrowing of money or the giving of any guarantee by the Government of India, or the amendment of the law with respect to any financial obligations undertaken or to be undertaken by the Government of India;
(c) the custody of the Consolidated Fund or the Contingency Fund of India, the payment of moneys into or the withdrawal of moneys from any such Fund;
(d) the appropriation of moneys out of the Consolidated Fund of India;
(e) the declaring of any expenditure to be expenditure charged on the Consolidated Fund of India or the increasing of the amount of any such expenditure;
(f) the receipt of money on account of the Consolidated Fund of India or the public account of India or the custody or issue of such money or the audit of the accounts of the Union or of a State;
(g) any matter incidental to any of the matters specified in sub-clauses (a) to (f).

Regarding the controversy surrounding the passage of Aadhaar, it was argued that the Aadhaar Act, 2016 was not a Money Bill because it included non-financial provisions, such as those related to privacy and data protection. Critics contended that the Aadhaar Act should have been passed through the regular legislative process, which would have required approval from both houses of parliament, rather than just the Lok Sabha (House of the People). This argument was based on the belief that the Aadhaar Act dealt with issues beyond mere financial matters, thus falling outside the definition of a Money Bill.

It is important to note that the controversy over the Aadhaar Act's classification as a Money Bill was a matter of debate and legal challenge, and it did not directly relate to the specific sections provided above. However, these sections outline the procedural requirements for Money Bills in the Indian legislative process.

According to Section 85 of the Consumer Protection Act, 2019, a product service provider can be held liable in a product liability action if:

(a) The service provided by him was faulty, imperfect, deficient, or inadequate in quality, nature, or manner of performance which is required to be provided by or under any law for the time being in force, or pursuant to any contract or otherwise;

(b) There was an act of omission or commission, negligence, or conscious withholding of any information which caused harm;

(c) The service provider did not issue adequate instructions or warnings to prevent any harm;

(d) The service did not conform to an express warranty or the terms and conditions of the contract.

Therefore, 'deficiency in service' refers to situations where a service provider fails to meet the standards expected under the law, contract, or industry norms, leading to a situation where the service provided is inadequate, harmful, or does not meet the promised quality or conditions.
